= Astragalus

_Scope: This entry treats_ astragalus _—_ Astragalus mongholicus _Bunge (= A. membranaceus var. mongholicus (Bunge) P.K. Hsiao), and the related A. membranaceus (Fisch. ex Link) Bunge — the canonical Chinese 黃芪 (huángqí, "yellow leader") of the_ Shennong Bencao Jing _top grade. The drug is the dried root. The genus Astragalus is the largest plant genus in the world (~3000 species), but only_ A. mongholicus _and_ A. membranaceus _are the canonical Chinese medicinal species; the Western_ A. propinquus _and many other species are different drugs. Status: classical and top-grade in Chinese medicine (_Shennong Bencao Jing _上品); not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman traditions (a Northeast Asian / Mongolian temperate-steppe herb); modern Western and global integrative-medicine use as a major qi-tonifying / immune-modulating adaptogen; well-documented modern pharmacology around the astragaloside saponins, polysaccharides, and isoflavonoids. One of the most clinically central herbs of Chinese medicine, alongside_ ginseng _(人參) and_ dang gui _(當歸)._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Astragalus mongholicus_ Bunge (preferred current taxon, the principal medicinal source) and *_A. membranaceus_* (Fisch. ex Link) Bunge (treated as conspecific or as a variant by various authorities), family Fabaceae (Leguminosae), subfamily Faboideae. The drug is the *dried root* (_Astragali radix_), harvested at 3–6 years from cultivated stock, sliced into characteristic horse-tongue-shaped pieces. The genus name *Astragalus* from Greek ἀστράγαλος (_astrágalos_), meaning *(1) the ankle bone / talus*, *(2) a knucklebone used in ancient gambling*, *(3) a plant — Theophrastus and Dioscorides use ἀστράγαλος for a class of legume-shaped knobby-rooted herbs* (probably including some southern European Astragalus, but the modern Northeast-Asian A. mongholicus is distinct). The "ankle bone" semantics relates to the knobby, jointed appearance of some Astragalus roots, or possibly to the seed shape — the etymology has been disputed.#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short (s.v. _astragalus_); Beekes (s.v. ἀστράγαλος); Theophrastus, _Historia Plantarum_, uses ἀστράγαλος for a class of leguminous herbs. The Greek classical use of ἀστράγαλος as a plant name *does not refer to the Chinese A. mongholicus* — flagged: the Latin botanical genus name is a modern (Linnaean / post-Linnaean) appropriation of the ancient Greek plant name, applied to a vast genus that includes the Mediterranean species the Greeks knew, the Northeast Asian medicinal species we treat here, and ~3000 others worldwide.]

*English:* _Astragalus_ — the botanical-Latin name in common use; *milk-vetch* — the broader English vernacular for the genus; *Mongolian milk-vetch* — for _A. mongholicus_ specifically; *huang qi* (or _huang-chi_, _huangqi_) — the Chinese name preserved in modern Western herbal usage. The word "milk-vetch" derives from the medieval European folk belief that goats and cows feeding on certain Astragalus species produced more milk — a parallel cross-system convergence with the Chinese classical use as a *qi tonifying* / strengthening drug (though the Western tradition's milk-vetch is a different species).#footnote[_OED_ (s.v. "milk-vetch", "astragalus"). The European milk-vetch / Astragalus folk tradition is documented for various European species; the Chinese 黃芪 is taxonomically distinct.]

*Chinese:* *黃芪 (huángqí)* — the canonical Chinese drug name, recorded in the *Shennong Bencao Jing* (神農本草經, Han dynasty) as a *top-grade drug (上品)*. The character 黃 huáng = "yellow" (the characteristic yellow internal color of the root); the character 芪 qí (older form 耆 qí, "elder, leader, eminent person, venerable") — the compound 黃芪 / 黃耆 thus literally "yellow leader / yellow eminent one" — emphasizing the herb's *high-status / leading role* among the qi-tonifying drugs. The older form 黃耆 is the form preserved in classical texts; the modern simplified form 黃芪 is the standard. The drug also known as *北芪 (běiqí)*, "northern qí" — emphasizing the northern (Inner Mongolian, Manchurian, North-Chinese) origin of the best-quality material.#footnote[黃芪 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (top grade 上品); systematized in _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The 北芪 designation for the high-quality Inner Mongolian / Manchurian product is preserved in modern Chinese herbal commerce — quality / origin matters.]

*Korean:* *황기 (hwanggi)* — Korean reading of the same characters; central in Korean traditional medicine and dietetics — *samgyetang* (삼계탕, ginseng-chicken soup, the Korean canonical summer-tonic dish) traditionally includes 황기 alongside 인삼 (insam, ginseng).

*Japanese:* *黄耆 (ōgi)* — Japanese reading; used in the kampō tradition; less centrally clinical than ginseng and the kampō-Chinese-medicine herb roster places 黄耆 as a tonic-support drug rather than a primary herb of Japanese herbal practice.

*Mongolian:* *хунчир (khunchir)* or transliterated names; the herb is genuinely Mongolian in geographic origin (Inner Mongolia, Outer Mongolia, the surrounding Manchurian-northern-Chinese steppe regions) — Mongolian folk-medical use is documented though less systematically codified than the Han Chinese register.

*Sanskrit / Arabic / Latin classical:* *Not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman traditions.* Despite the genus name being a Greek classical word, the canonical medicinal Chinese astragalus species is a Northeast Asian / Mongolian temperate-steppe herb outside the geographical range of the Indian, Arabic-Islamic, and Mediterranean classical pharmacopoeias.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*黃芪 is one of the most canonical and most clinically central Chinese drugs* — top-grade in the *Shennong Bencao Jing*, central in countless classical and modern formulas, and treated as *the principal qi-tonifying drug of the Spleen and Lung channels* (alongside ginseng, with which it shares the qi-tonifying role but with distinctive differences — see below).#footnote[黃芪 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_; elaborated extensively in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Slightly Warm (微溫).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Lung (肺), Spleen (脾).

*Distinction from ginseng (人參):* astragalus and ginseng are the *two principal qi tonics* of the Chinese pharmacy, but they have distinct profiles:
- *Ginseng (人參)* — tonifies *Spleen-Lung qi* with stronger *original qi (元氣 yuán qì)* action; more *warming and yang-rousing*; suited to *acute exhaustion, severe vacuity, post-illness collapse*; *more expensive and more reserved*.
- *Astragalus (黃芪)* — tonifies *Spleen-Lung qi* with stronger *surface qi (衛氣 wèi qì)* action; more *raising-yang (升陽) and surface-stabilizing*; suited to *chronic mild vacuity, immune weakness, sinking-and-prolapse patterns*; *less expensive and used in much larger doses* (15–60 g per day routinely, versus 3–10 g of ginseng).

The two are often *combined* in qi-tonifying formulas, with the dose-balance and the formula architecture differing for the clinical situation.

*Actions & indications:*
- *補氣升陽 (bǔ qì shēng yáng)* — *tonifies qi and raises yang* — the cardinal action; for Spleen-qi vacuity with fatigue, poor appetite, loose stools; *sinking-of-middle-qi (中氣下陷)* patterns with organ prolapse (gastric, uterine, rectal); chronic diarrhea. The famous *補中益氣湯 (Bǔ Zhōng Yì Qì Tāng, "Tonify the Middle and Augment Qi Decoction")* of Li Dongyuan is the canonical formula for this action.
- *固表止汗 (gù biǎo zhǐ hàn)* — *stabilizes the surface and stops sweating* — for spontaneous sweating (qi-vacuity), low resistance to wind, and frequent colds. The famous *玉屏風散 (Yù Píng Fēng Sǎn, "Jade Windscreen Powder")* of Zhu Zhenheng — 黃芪 + 白朮 + 防風 — is the canonical surface-stabilizing formula, widely used for *immune support and recurrent upper respiratory infection prevention* in modern Chinese clinical practice.
- *利水消腫 (lì shuǐ xiāo zhǒng)* — *promotes urination, reduces edema* — for edema from qi-vacuity (the diuresis works *through* the qi-tonifying action — the Chinese physiology recognizes that qi vacuity allows fluid stagnation).
- *托瘡生肌 (tuō chuāng shēng jī)* — *expresses sores and generates flesh* — for chronic non-healing wounds, sores that fail to drain, lingering infections from qi vacuity. *黃芪 promotes wound healing* through the qi-tonifying action; modern dermatology and surgery uses standardized astragalus extracts for this purpose.
- *養血生血 (yǎng xuè shēng xuè)* — *nourishes and generates blood* — when combined with blood-tonifying drugs (especially 當歸 dang gui), augments the blood-tonifying action. The *當歸補血湯 (Dāng Guī Bǔ Xuè Tāng)* — *huángqí* + *dāngguī* in a 5:1 ratio — is one of the great blood-tonifying formulas, used for severe blood vacuity from chronic disease, postpartum care, and chemotherapy support.
- *Modern immune-modulating register:* 黃芪 is extensively used in modern Chinese clinical oncology, infectious disease, and chronic illness contexts as an *immunomodulatory* drug; the polysaccharide fraction (APS, astragalus polysaccharides) is the principal active fraction for this register, and standardized APS preparations are licensed pharmaceuticals in China.

*Cautions:* externally-contracted febrile disease (the tonifying action would lock in pathogen); excess heat patterns; food stagnation; severe yin vacuity with internal heat; concurrent acute infection with strong-heat-pattern manifestations. *The principal classical contraindication is the acute-external-pathogen pattern* — astragalus is for chronic / vacuity conditions, not acute illness.#footnote[Actions and cautions: 黃芪 in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. The acute-vs-chronic distinction is the classical clinical commonplace.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Astragalus is not classical in Ayurveda._ The reading by extension would place the herb in the *bṛṃhaṇa-balya-rasāyana* register — strengthening, anabolic, rejuvenating; the *sweet-mildly-warming-snigdha* profile maps cleanly to a *Vāta-pacifying, Kapha-aggravating at heavy dose, Pitta-neutral* doṣa effect. Modern post-classical Ayurvedic integrative use of astragalus alongside classical Ayurvedic adaptogens (ashwagandha, śatāvarī) — *post-classical extension only*, flagged.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Astragalus is not classical in Tibb._ No Avicennan, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Tibb al-Nabawī attestation. Some Middle Eastern Astragalus species are used in regional Tibb folk medicine (different species, primarily for the gum resin _kathīrāʾ_ / tragacanth from _A. gummifer_, a separate drug), but the Chinese medicinal _A. mongholicus_ is outside the classical Tibb canon.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

- *補中益氣湯 (Bǔ Zhōng Yì Qì Tāng, "Tonify the Middle and Augment Qi Decoction")* — Li Dongyuan, _Pi Wei Lun_ (12th c.); 黃芪 (君 sovereign) + 人參 + 白朮 + 甘草 + 當歸 + 陳皮 + 升麻 + 柴胡. For Spleen-qi vacuity, sinking-of-middle-qi, prolapse, chronic fatigue. *The foundational Li Dongyuan qi-raising formula*.
- *玉屏風散 (Yù Píng Fēng Sǎn, "Jade Windscreen Powder")* — Zhu Zhenheng / _Dan Xi Xin Fa_; 黃芪 + 白朮 + 防風. For surface qi vacuity with spontaneous sweating and susceptibility to colds. *The canonical Chinese immune-support formula*; widely used in modern integrative medicine for recurrent upper respiratory infection prevention.
- *當歸補血湯 (Dāng Guī Bǔ Xuè Tāng, "Dang Gui Tonify-Blood Decoction")* — Li Dongyuan; 黃芪 + 當歸 in 5:1 ratio. For severe qi-blood vacuity; the classical demonstration that *blood is generated from qi*.
- *黃芪建中湯 (Huáng Qí Jiàn Zhōng Tāng, "Astragalus Construct-the-Middle Decoction")* — Zhang Zhongjing, _Jin Gui Yao Lue_; 黃芪 added to the classical 小建中湯 (Minor Construct-the-Middle Decoction); for chronic Spleen-Stomach qi vacuity with cold abdominal pain.
- *防己黃芪湯 (Fáng Jǐ Huáng Qí Tāng)* — _Jin Gui Yao Lue_; for damp obstruction with surface qi vacuity, edema.
- *補陽還五湯 (Bǔ Yáng Huán Wǔ Tāng, "Tonify Yang and Restore Five Tenths Decoction")* — Wang Qingren, _Yi Lin Gai Cuo_ (Qing dynasty); large dose of 黃芪 (120 g) with blood-moving herbs; for post-stroke hemiplegia with qi vacuity blood stasis. Modern Chinese stroke rehabilitation makes substantial use of this formula and its derivatives.
- *Modern compound preparations* — 黃芪 is in numerous modern Chinese-pharmacopoeia listings for immune support, chronic fatigue, post-chemotherapy support, and chronic kidney disease.
- *Dietetic preparations:* 黃芪燉雞 (astragalus-stewed chicken), 黃芪粥 (astragalus congee), 黃芪茶 (astragalus tea) — daily-tonic preparations.#footnote[Formula sources: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; Li Dongyuan, _Pi Wei Lun_; Zhang Zhongjing, _Jin Gui Yao Lue_; Wang Qingren, _Yi Lin Gai Cuo_; modern Chinese-pharmacopoeia preparations.]

=== Korean

- *Samgyetang (삼계탕)* — traditional Korean ginseng-chicken summer-tonic soup; *황기* is included alongside *인삼* (ginseng), garlic, jujubes, sweet rice, and a young chicken. Eaten on the hottest days of summer (the Korean dietetic logic: *to support the body's exterior qi when sweating most strongly*).

=== Western / Greco-Roman, Ayurveda, Ṭibb

No classical formulas. Modern Western herbal integrative use of astragalus extracts for immune support is post-classical (1980s onward).

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Astragalus is a *cool-dry continental steppe / mountain-grassland* perennial — native to *Inner Mongolia, Outer Mongolia, the Manchurian-northern-Chinese steppe, and the Korean and Japanese cool-temperate mountain regions*. The plant tolerates cold winters and is adapted to the continental seasonal-extreme climate with cool-dry-steppe character. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Cool-dry continental / steppe (the home climate)

Cold-dry winters; cool-warm summers; constitutional pressure on Vāta-Kapha (climates.typ); chronic Spleen-qi load.

*Traditional preparation.* The Chinese classical and modern pharmaceutical register treats 黃芪 as a *decoctional drug in compound formulas*; the Korean dietetic register includes it in chicken-soup preparations; the Mongolian folk register uses it as a daily-tonic herb. The cool-dry continental steppe climate is precisely the climate the herb is constitutionally suited to — the warming-tonifying-surface-stabilizing action is climatically apt for the chronic cold-wind exposure of the steppe.

*Cares.* The qi-tonifying register is universally applicable; the surface-stabilizing register is particularly apt for *cold-windy steppe and continental climates*. Hot-humid summer use should be moderated (the warming action can compound summer heat).

=== Other climates — post-classical use

Modern Western use of astragalus extract is climatically agnostic. The traditional Chinese-Korean use anchors to cool-season constitutional support.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern Western adaptogen / immune-support register has globalized astragalus use; the classical Chinese acute-illness contraindication (*do not use during acute infection*) is sometimes lost in the modern "general immune booster" framing. *The classical use is for chronic vacuity, not acute illness*; the Western popular framing of astragalus as a cold-fighter is sometimes counter-classical. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (dried root):*
- *Astragalus polysaccharides (APS)* — high molecular weight glucans and heteroglycans; *the principal immunomodulatory fraction*; multiple subfractions (APS-I, APS-II, etc.) with documented immunomodulatory activity (macrophage activation, T-cell support, NK-cell modulation).
- *Astragaloside saponins* — *astragaloside IV* is the principal compound (the canonical reference saponin); also astragalosides I-VIII, cycloastragenol (the aglycone), isoastragalosides; documented cardioprotective, anti-fibrotic, and anti-aging effects.
- *Isoflavones and isoflavonoids* — formononetin, calycosin, calycosin-7-O-β-D-glucoside, ononin; antioxidant and modest phytoestrogenic activity.
- *Phenolic acids:* ferulic acid, coumarins.
- *Sterols:* β-sitosterol, daucosterol.
- *Amino acids:* including the unusual γ-aminobutyric acid (GABA) at modest concentrations.
- *Trace elements:* selenium (modest), modest other minerals.
- *Cycloastragenol* — *the aglycone of astragaloside IV*; widely studied as a *telomerase activator* — has spawned a commercial supplement industry on the basis of preclinical telomere-extending activity; clinical relevance to longevity is *preliminary and overstated in popular marketing* — flagged.#footnote[Phytochemistry: extensive modern pharmacognosy; standard references in _Phytochemistry_, _Planta Medica_, _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_; the astragaloside IV and APS fractions are the principal characterized active fractions. Cycloastragenol and telomerase: preclinical data are real but clinical translation is preliminary; the supplement marketing substantially exceeds the evidence.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Immunomodulation — the headline action*. Astragalus polysaccharides (APS) and saponins modulate macrophage, T-cell, NK-cell, and dendritic-cell function; documented effects include enhanced phagocytosis, modulated cytokine production, and improved response to vaccination. Modern Chinese clinical use in oncology (chemotherapy support — reducing leukopenia, supporting immune recovery), infectious disease, and chronic immune dysregulation is substantial. Western clinical translation in trials of chronic immune deficiency, recurrent infection, and post-chemotherapy support shows modest-to-favorable results.
- *Cardiovascular* — astragaloside IV has *significant cardioprotective activity* in preclinical models; clinical use in modern Chinese cardiology for congestive heart failure, post-MI rehabilitation, and arrhythmia management; multiple small Chinese RCTs support adjunctive use, though Western cardiology evidence is more limited.
- *Anti-fibrotic / nephroprotective* — astragaloside IV has documented anti-fibrotic activity in kidney, liver, and lung; modern Chinese nephrology uses astragalus preparations for chronic kidney disease, diabetic nephropathy, and other chronic kidney conditions; clinical trial evidence is suggestive though modest.
- *Anti-aging / telomerase activation* — preclinical; cycloastragenol activates telomerase in some cell systems; *clinical translation to human longevity is preliminary and the commercial supplement marketing is ahead of the evidence* — flagged.
- *Anti-tumor* — preclinical; clinical use as adjunctive (not primary) cancer treatment in modern Chinese oncology.
- *Antiviral* — preclinical activity against various respiratory viruses; clinical use during COVID-19 was extensive in Chinese integrative medicine, with preliminary supportive data.
- *Antioxidant and anti-inflammatory* — substantial preclinical.
- *Hypoglycemic and lipid-modulating* — modest preclinical and clinical effects.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Chinese decoction:* 9–30 g per day in standard practice; *15–60 g per day* in modern Chinese clinical practice for serious indications; high-dose use (30–120 g) in some chronic-disease protocols, e.g. post-stroke rehabilitation per 補陽還五湯.
- *Standardized extract:* 250–500 mg of standardized extract per day (Western herbalism).
- *APS / polysaccharide preparation:* 200–1000 mg per day in modern Chinese pharmacology.
- *Tincture:* 1–4 mL of 1:5 tincture, 2–3 times daily.
- *Tea:* 5–10 g of dried root per cup, decocted for 20–30 min, 1–2 cups per day.
- *Pregnancy:* astragalus is generally considered safe at moderate doses; some sources advise caution at therapeutic doses; verify with obstetric / TCM clinician.
- *Concurrent medication:* immunosuppressants (theoretical antagonism — the immunomodulatory action could counteract intentional immunosuppression in transplant medicine, autoimmune disease therapy; *significant clinical caveat for transplant patients*); antihypertensives (modest additive effect — astragalus has modest BP-lowering effect); diabetes medications (modest additive hypoglycemic effect); concurrent acute febrile illness (classical contraindication preserved).
- *Acute febrile illness:* avoid (classical contraindication).
- *Heat-pattern conditions, severe yin vacuity with internal heat:* moderate.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.* The immunomodulatory action in transplant patients and autoimmune patients on immunosuppressive therapy is the principal modern pharmacokinetic concern.#footnote[Dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; modern Chinese clinical literature; AHP _Astragalus Monograph_. Immunomodulation and transplant interaction: documented in modern integrative-medicine literature; clinical caveat for solid-organ transplant patients on immunosuppressive therapy is real.]

